2026-06-30 15:08:04,949 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-d6a90b90-8d18-40e8-a9b7-4004e4fdb5ce', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nOT_160929_CBarnes_BirA_BHC80-02.raw\nOT_160929_CBarnes_BirA_BHC80-03.raw\nOT_160929_CBarnes_BirA_CoREST1-01.raw\nOT_160929_CBarnes_BirA_CoREST1-02.raw\nOT_160929_CBarnes_BirA_CoREST1-03.raw\nOT_160929_CBarnes_BirA_HDAC1-01.raw\nOT_160929_CBarnes_BirA_HDAC1-02.raw\nOT_160929_CBarnes_BirA_HDAC1-03.raw\nOT_160929_CBarnes_BirA_HDAC1-04.raw\nOT_160929_CBarnes_BirA_LSD1-01.raw\nOT_160929_CBarnes_BirA_LSD1-02.raw\nOT_160929_CBarnes_BirA_LSD1-03.raw\nOT_160929_CBarnes_BirA_LSD1-04.raw\nOT_160929_CBarnes_BirA_NLS-01.raw\nOT_160929_CBarnes_BirA_NLS-02.raw\nOT_160929_CBarnes_BirA_NLS-03.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_LSD1-0hr_B10.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_LSD1-0hr_B11.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_LSD1-0hr_B8.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_LSD1-0hr_B8b.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_LSD1-24hr_B10.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_LSD1-24hr_B11.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_LSD1-24hr_B8.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_LSD1-24hr_B8b.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_LSD1-66hr_B10.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_LSD1-66hr_B11.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_LSD1-66hr_B8.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_LSD1-66hr_B8b.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_NLS-0hr_D1.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_NLS-0hr_D12.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_NLS-0hr_D1b.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_NLS-0hr_D2.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_NLS-24hr_D1.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_NLS-24hr_D12.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_NLS-24hr_D1b.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_NLS-24hr_D2.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_NLS-66hr_D1.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_NLS-66hr_D12.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_NLS-66hr_D1b.raw\nOT_170111_CBarnes_BirA_NLS-66hr_D2.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nProximity-dependent biotin identification (BioID) reveals a dynamic LSD1-CoREST interactome during embryonic stem cell differentiation\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nLysine specific demethylase 1 (LSD1/KDM1A) regulates gene expression as part of the CoREST complex, along with co-repressor of REST (CoREST) and histone deacetylase 1 (HDAC1). CoREST is recruited to specific genomic loci by components of the core complex and numerous transient interactions with chromatin associated factors and transcription factors. To sample the chromatin environment in proximity to CoREST, we performed proximity-dependent biotin-identification (BioID) with four different members of the complex in 293T cells. Retaining only targets identified with 3 out of 4 baits, we identified 302 CoREST-associated proteins. Among this group were 16 of 18 known CoREST components and numerous novel associations, including readers (CHD3, 4, 6, 7 and 8), writers (KMT2B and KMT2D) and erasers (KDM2B) of histone methylation. However, components of other HDAC1 containing complexes (e.g. Sin3A, NuRD) were largely absent, suggesting that CoREST functions independently. As LSD1 plays an essential role in early embryonic development, we performed BioID using the endogeneously tagged protein in pluripotent, early- and late-differentiating embryonic stem cells. We identified 157 LSD1-associated proteins of which 66 were constitutively associated across all three time-points (44%), including novel interactions with the MMB and ChAHP complexes. These data imply that the majority of CoREST interactions are dynamic and highly cell type dependent.\n\n[METHODS]\nBioID BirA* constructs were generated and transfected in to cells as described above. Where transient transfections were used two 10 cm plates of HEK293T cells were transfected and 24 hours later 50\uf06dM biotin was added to the culture media for 24 hours prior to harvesting. Stably transfected Lsd1lox/\uf0443 ESCs expressing either LSD1-BirA* or NLS-BirA* (generated as described above) were plated on to 15cm culture plates 24 hours prior to being treated with 50\uf06dM biotin for 18 hours before harvesting. Cells were harvested by scraping in to 2ml of PBS before pelleting through centrifugation at 1100 rpm for 3 minutes. Pellets were lysed in 1.5ml of BioID lysis buffer (50mM Tris-HCl pH8.0, 150mM NaCl, 0.5% NP-40, 0.5% Triton-X100, 0.1% SDS), and rotated at 4°C for 20 minutes. Samples were then sonicated for 4 minutes (30 seconds on/30 seconds off), using the Bioruptor Pico Sonication device. Samples were cleared by centrifugation at 14000rpm at 4˚C for 15 minutes. Extracts were then added to 60\uf06dl of Streptavidin agarose beads (Novagen; 69203-3) that had been washed 3 times in BioID lysis buffer. Extracts were incubated with the beads overnight whilst rotating at 4°C. Beads were washed for 10 minutes in Wash Buffer 1 (2% SDS in ddH2O), Wash Buffer 2 (Deoxycholate 0.2%, Triton X-100 1%, NaCl 500mM, EDTA 1mM, HEPES pH 7.5 50mM), Wash Buffer 3 (Tris pH8 10mM, LiCl 250mM, NP-40 0.5%, Deoxycholate 0.5%, Triton X-100 1%, NaCl 500mM, EDTA 1mM), then twice in Wash Buffer 4 (Tris pH 7.4 50mM, NaCl 50mM). Streptavidin bead IPs were washed twice in 50mM ammonium bicarbonate, then resuspended in 200μl of ammonium bicarbonate, reduced with with 10mM Tri(2-carboxyethyl) phosphine hydrochloride (TCEP) and alkylated with 20mM in the dark at 37°C, for 30 minutes. 1μg of trypsin (Pierce, MS grade) was added to each sample and incubated whilst shaking overnight at 25 °C. Following trypsin digestion, streptavidin beads were pelleted and supernatants were acidified with trifluroacetic acid and desalted using  C18 spin columns (ThermoFisher)  were used according to manufacturer’s protocol. Samples were re-suspended in 40μl of 0.5% formaic acid analysed by nanoflow LC‐MS/MS using an Orbitrap Elite (Thermo Fisher) hybrid mass spectrometer equipped with a nanospray source, coupled to an Ultimate RSLCnano LC System (Dionex). The system was controlled by Xcalibur 3.0.61 (Thermo Fisher) and DCMSLink (Dionex). Peptides were desalted on‐line using an Acclaim PepMap 100 C18 nano/capillary BioLC, 100A nanoViper 20mm x 75µm I.D. particle size 3µm (Fisher Scientific) and then separated using a 125‐min gradient from 5 to 35% buffer B (0.5% formic acid in 80% acetonitrile) on an EASY‐Spray column, 50 cm × 50 μm ID, PepMap C18, 2 μm particles, 100 Å pore size (Fisher Scientific). The LTQ‐Orbitrap Elite was operated with a cycle of one MS (in the Orbitrap) acquired at a resolution of 60,000 at m/z 400, with the top 20 most abundant multiply charged (2+ and higher) ions in a given chromatographic window subjected to MS/MS fragmentation in the linear ion trap. An FTMS target value of 1e6 and an ion trap MSn target value of 1e4 were used with the lock mass (445.120025) enabled. Maximum FTMS scan accumulation time of 500 ms and maximum ion trap MSn scan accumulation time of 100 ms were used. Dynamic exclusion was enabled with a repeat duration of 45 s with an exclusion list of 500 and an exclusion duration of 30 s.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human); Mus musculus (mouse)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
