(K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:09317), DSPU (K,nterm), DSSBU (K,nterm),\n    DHSO (carbonyl-reactive D,E; AC=XLMOD:02226), BMSO (cysteine-specific; C),\n    SDASO (K,nterm; photo+MS-cleavable), SDASO-L (K,nterm; photo+MS-cleavable),\n    SSD (K,nterm), CBDPS (K,nterm), iQlnk (K,nterm)\n  PIR-type enrichable (reporter-ion, NOT backbone MS-cleavable): PIR (K,nterm), BDP-NHP (K,nterm)\n  Common non-cleavable: BS3/BS2G (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02000/02005), DSS (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02001), EDC (D,E,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02010), formaldehyde (K,nterm)\n  IMAC-enrichable (non-cleavable): PhoX (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02258), pDSPE (K,nterm; AC=null)\n  Photo-crosslinkers (non-cleavable, UV-activated): diazirine (K or incorporated aa), benzophenone (Bpa, pBpa; C or incorporated aa), SDA/sulfo-SDA (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02134/02136), aryl azide\n  NOTE: Do NOT extract proximity labeling enzymes (TurboID, BioID, APEX, biotin-phenol+H2O2) as crosslinkers — these are NOT chemical cross-linking reagents.\n\nterm: crosslinker concentration\nsdrf_column: comment[crosslinker concentration]\nontology: PRIDE:0000951\ndescription: Concentration of the crosslinking reagent used in the reaction.\npattern: number + mM or uM (e.g. "1 mM", "500 uM", "0.5 mM")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: crosslinker to protein ratio\nsdrf_column: comment[crosslinker to protein ratio]\nontology: PRIDE:0000952\ndescription: Molar ratio of crosslinker to protein in the reaction.\npattern: ratio expression (e.g. "50:1", "100-fold molar excess", "5 mM per 1 mg/mL")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: quenching reagent\nsdrf_column: comment[quenching reagent]\nontology: PRIDE:0000953\ndescription: Reagent used to stop, quench, neutralize, terminate, or consume the crosslinking reaction.\ninstructions:\n  Extract only if explicitly stated in the paper, PRIDE metadata, or cited protocol text.\n  Search for synonyms including: quenched, stopped, terminated, neutralized, blocked,\n  residual crosslinker, washed with, incubated with, reaction was halted.\nCommon quenchers:\n  - NHS ester crosslinkers such as BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU: Tris, ammonium bicarbonate, glycine, lysine\n  - Formaldehyde crosslinking: glycine or Tris are common, but only annotate if explicitly stated\nFor formaldehyde datasets:\n  - Inspect the 3–5 sentences after the formaldehyde crosslinking sentence.\n  - If the text says "as described previously" or "as described before", and no quencher is named, attempt to read the cited artical and extract the quenching reagent from its methods section.\n    If that fall back failes then return value=null with confidence="low" and reasoning="quenching reagent not explicitly stated in provided text; may require cited protocol."\n  - Do NOT infer glycine solely because formaldehyde was used.\n  - Do NOT treat PBS washes alone as a quenching reagent.\n  - Treat Tris-containing lysis buffer as a possible quencher only if the text clearly implies it was use\n  \nterm: crosslink distance\nsdrf_column: characteristics[crosslink distance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000948\ndescription: Maximum Cα–Cα distance constraint (spacer arm length) provided by the crosslinker.\npattern: number + Å (e.g. "11.4 Å", "30 Å", "~27 Å")\ninstructions: Extract the stated spacer length or max distance. Common values:\n  BS3/DSS ≈ 11.4 Å, DSSO ≈ 10.1 Å, DSBU ≈ 12.5 Å, EDC ≈ 0 Å (zero-length).\n  Return null if not explicitly stated in the text.\n\nterm: chemical cross-linking coupled with ms\nsdrf_column: comment[chemical cross-linking coupled with ms]\nontology: PRIDE:0000430\ndescription: MS-based cross-linking methodology identifier.\nallowed_values: cross-linking mass spectrometry\ninstructions: Always return "cross-linking mass spectrometry" for any XL-MS dataset.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null). For comment[cross-linker] the\n           "value" field should contain the full NT=...;AC=...;CL=...;TA=... string,\n           and also include separate sub-fields: "NT", "AC", "CL", "TA".\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null\n\nExample for comment[cross-linker]:\n{\n  "comment[cross-linker]": {\n    "value": "NT=DSSO;AC=XLMOD:02056;CL=yes;TA=K,S,T,Y",\n    "NT": "DSSO", "AC": "XLMOD:02056", "CL": "yes", "TA": "K,S,T,Y",\n    "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "crosslinked with 1 mM DSSO",\n    "evidence_location": "Methods"\n  }\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nProximity-dependent biotin identification (BioID) reveals a dynamic LSD1-CoREST interactome during embryonic stem cell differentiation\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nLysine specific demethylase 1 (LSD1/KDM1A) regulates gene expression as part of the CoREST complex, along with co-repressor of REST (CoREST) and histone deacetylase 1 (HDAC1). CoREST is recruited to specific genomic loci by components of the core complex and numerous transient interactions with chromatin associated factors and transcription factors. To sample the chromatin environment in proximity to CoREST, we performed proximity-dependent biotin-identification (BioID) with four different members of the complex in 293T cells. Retaining only targets identified with 3 out of 4 baits, we identified 302 CoREST-associated proteins. Among this group were 16 of 18 known CoREST components and numerous novel associations, including readers (CHD3, 4, 6, 7 and 8), writers (KMT2B and KMT2D) and erasers (KDM2B) of histone methylation. However, components of other HDAC1 containing complexes (e.g. Sin3A, NuRD) were largely absent, suggesting that CoREST functions independently. As LSD1 plays an essential role in early embryonic development, we performed BioID using the endogeneously tagged protein in pluripotent, early- and late-differentiating embryonic stem cells. We identified 157 LSD1-associated proteins of which 66 were constitutively associated across all three time-points (44%), including novel interactions with the MMB and ChAHP complexes. These data imply that the majority of CoREST interactions are dynamic and highly cell type dependent.\n\n[METHODS]\nBioID BirA* constructs were generated and transfected in to cells as described above. Where transient transfections were used two 10 cm plates of HEK293T cells were transfected and 24 hours later 50\uf06dM biotin was added to the culture media for 24 hours prior to harvesting. Stably transfected Lsd1lox/\uf0443 ESCs expressing either LSD1-BirA* or NLS-BirA* (generated as described above) were plated on to 15cm culture plates 24 hours prior to being treated with 50\uf06dM biotin for 18 hours before harvesting. Cells were harvested by scraping in to 2ml of PBS before pelleting through centrifugation at 1100 rpm for 3 minutes. Pellets were lysed in 1.5ml of BioID lysis buffer (50mM Tris-HCl pH8.0, 150mM NaCl, 0.5% NP-40, 0.5% Triton-X100, 0.1% SDS), and rotated at 4°C for 20 minutes. Samples were then sonicated for 4 minutes (30 seconds on/30 seconds off), using the Bioruptor Pico Sonication device. Samples were cleared by centrifugation at 14000rpm at 4˚C for 15 minutes. Extracts were then added to 60\uf06dl of Streptavidin agarose beads (Novagen; 69203-3) that had been washed 3 times in BioID lysis buffer. Extracts were incubated with the beads overnight whilst rotating at 4°C. Beads were washed for 10 minutes in Wash Buffer 1 (2% SDS in ddH2O), Wash Buffer 2 (Deoxycholate 0.2%, Triton X-100 1%, NaCl 500mM, EDTA 1mM, HEPES pH 7.5 50mM), Wash Buffer 3 (Tris pH8 10mM, LiCl 250mM, NP-40 0.5%, Deoxycholate 0.5%, Triton X-100 1%, NaCl 500mM, EDTA 1mM), then twice in Wash Buffer 4 (Tris pH 7.4 50mM, NaCl 50mM). Streptavidin bead IPs were washed twice in 50mM ammonium bicarbonate, then resuspended in 200μl of ammonium bicarbonate, reduced with with 10mM Tri(2-carboxyethyl) phosphine hydrochloride (TCEP) and alkylated with 20mM in the dark at 37°C, for 30 minutes. 1μg of trypsin (Pierce, MS grade) was added to each sample and incubated whilst shaking overnight at 25 °C. Following trypsin digestion, streptavidin beads were pelleted and supernatants were acidified with trifluroacetic acid and desalted using  C18 spin columns (ThermoFisher)  were used according to manufacturer’s protocol. Samples were re-suspended in 40μl of 0.5% formaic acid analysed by nanoflow LC‐MS/MS using an Orbitrap Elite (Thermo Fisher) hybrid mass spectrometer equipped with a nanospray source, coupled to an Ultimate RSLCnano LC System (Dionex). The system was controlled by Xcalibur 3.0.61 (Thermo Fisher) and DCMSLink (Dionex). Peptides were desalted on‐line using an Acclaim PepMap 100 C18 nano/capillary BioLC, 100A nanoViper 20mm x 75µm I.D. particle size 3µm (Fisher Scientific) and then separated using a 125‐min gradient from 5 to 35% buffer B (0.5% formic acid in 80% acetonitrile) on an EASY‐Spray column, 50 cm × 50 μm ID, PepMap C18, 2 μm particles, 100 Å pore size (Fisher Scientific). The LTQ‐Orbitrap Elite was operated with a cycle of one MS (in the Orbitrap) acquired at a resolution of 60,000 at m/z 400, with the top 20 most abundant multiply charged (2+ and higher) ions in a given chromatographic window subjected to MS/MS fragmentation in the linear ion trap. An FTMS target value of 1e6 and an ion trap MSn target value of 1e4 were used with the lock mass (445.120025) enabled. Maximum FTMS scan accumulation time of 500 ms and maximum ion trap MSn scan accumulation time of 100 ms were used. Dynamic exclusion was enabled with a repeat duration of 45 s with an exclusion list of 500 and an exclusion duration of 30 s.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
